<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fu Jian (337–385)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/337">337</a>–385</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Fu Jian of Former Qin, Emperor Xuanzhao of Former Qin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fu Jian was the Di ruler of the Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> from 357, served by the able minister Wang Meng. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a>, Former Liang and Dai, unifying northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 376. His invasion of the southern Eastern Jin ended in disaster at the Battle of the Fei River in 383, after which his empire broke apart, and he was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-chang">Yao Chang</a> in 385.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fu-jian-337-385.jpg" alt="Fu Jian (337–385)" width="320" height="507" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: see below · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harvard_drs_49802792_%E7%B9%A1%E5%83%8F%E4%B8%89%E5%9C%8B%E6%BC%94%E7%BE%A9%E7%BA%8C%E7%B7%A8_v.1_(%E8%8B%BB%E5%A0%85).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fu Jian was born in 337 to Fu Xiong and Lady Gou, when the family name was still Pu. His grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-hong">Pu Hong</a>, a Di chieftain and general of Later Zhao, took the name Fu, and his uncle Fu Jian (317–355) founded Former Qin in 351. Under his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-sheng">Fu Sheng</a>, whose rule was violent and capricious, he helped defeat the Qiang chief Yao Xiang in 357 and persuaded the commander to spare Yao's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-chang">Yao Chang</a>. Warned that Fu Sheng meant to kill him, he attacked the palace with his brother Fu Fa, put Fu Sheng to death and took the title Heavenly King rather than emperor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>With Wang Meng as his chief minister, Fu Jian established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a>, encouraged officials to recommend able men and held them responsible for those they named. He repeatedly spared rebels and defeated enemies. In 369 his troops helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> defeat the Jin general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen">Huan Wen</a>, and when Yan withheld the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> region it had promised, Wang Meng led 60,000 men against it and destroyed it in 370. Fu Jian pardoned its emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-wei">Murong Wei</a> and moved many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> region. In 373 he took the Jin lands of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>.</p><p>Wang Meng died in 375, advising him on his deathbed to stop the wars against Jin and to trust the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> and Qiang officials less; Fu Jian followed neither counsel. His later campaigns drained the empire. In 380 he spread his Di people, a small minority, across the provinces, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> full of Xianbei and Qiang. Against most of his officials he invaded Jin in 383, boasting that his soldiers' whips could stop the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the defeat at the Fei River, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a> escorted the wounded Fu Jian back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> but then founded Later Yan, and Murong Hong began Western Yan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a>. When Fu Jian killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-chang">Yao Chang</a>'s messengers, Yao fled and founded Later Qin. Besieged and starving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, Fu Jian led an army out to find food, and the city fell behind him. Captured near Wujiang Mountain and held at Xinping, he refused to pass the throne to Yao and killed his own daughters Fu Bao and Fu Jin to spare them humiliation, before Yao's soldiers strangled him. Traditional histories judged him virtuous and just, but held that sparing too many enemies brought about his downfall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fu Jian (337–385)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 376 the Former Qin ruler <b>Fu Jian</b> invaded, Shiyijian died amid family violence, and Dai was divided.</blockquote><a href="/state/dai-sixteen-kingdoms">Dai (Sixteen Kingdoms)</a></li><li><blockquote>In 376 the Former Qin ruler <b>Fu Jian</b> sent an army and Zhang Tianxi surrendered, taking a post at the Qin court.</blockquote><a href="/state/former-liang">Former Liang</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Fu Jian</b> (the elder) took Chang'an in 351 and proclaimed the state of Qin.</blockquote><a href="/state/former-qin">Former Qin</a></li><li><blockquote>Yao Chang, a Qiang general of Former Qin, rebelled after the Fei River defeat in 383, captured and killed the Qin ruler <b>Fu Jian</b> in 385 and proclaimed himself emperor of Qin in 386, with his capital at Chang'an.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-qin">Later Qin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.33,108.71&amp;year=368" aria-label="Xianyang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7315 920 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Fu Jian (337–385)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7765 1190h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7765 1190h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.33,108.71&amp;year=368">What was here in 368 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
